# Build artefacts - everything here is regenerated by `make`
*.o
*.elf
*.bin
bin/
dist/

# Generated shellcode headers. bin2c.py turns each blob into a C array, and the
# shellcode Makefiles write the result next to it; the payload includes them, and the
# top-level build runs the shellcode first so they always exist by then. Only these
# two are generated - boot_linux.h, utils.h, shellcode_hv_args.h and break_hv.h are
# written by hand.
shellcode/hv/shellcode_hv.h
shellcode/hv_takeover/takeover.h

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Kernel and hypervisor dumps. Large - the 13.60 kernel is 46 MB and the
# hypervisor 4 MB - and they are inputs to the tools, not build artefacts: they
# are read from the console but never produced by `make`. Kept out of the tree so
# they cannot be committed by accident, and so `make clean` and checkouts leave
# them alone. tools/verify-offsets.py expects:
#   dumps/kernel-13.60-full.bin   dumps/kernel-13.60-ext.bin   dumps/hv-13.60.bin
dumps/

# Python bytecode from the tools.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Development tools, kept out of the repository on purpose.
#
# This repository is the 13.60 payload: the sources, the shellcode and what is
# needed to build and send them. tools/ and payload/ are the workshop that was
# used to get here - the offset verifier, the xref and function scanners, and the
# payloads that pulled the kernel and hypervisor dumps. They are useless without
# dumps/, which is already ignored because it is 70 MB of images, so the whole
# workshop lives together on the machine it was built on rather than in the tree.
#
# Nothing here is needed to build the payload or to run it. If you are looking
# for them, they are on the machine that made the port, alongside dumps/.
tools/
payload/
